//BUG="only the literal `true` should exempt a loop, but no loop is checked at all"
//ERR any

// `1 == 1` is always true, but it is an expression, not the literal `true`, and
// 05_functions/29_while_true_returns_from_inside.cpl states the rule the check
// is meant to follow: the exemption is syntactic, and a condition that merely
// happens to always hold is still assumed to fall through. Constant-folding
// conditions to decide reachability would put the compiler in the business of
// proving termination.
//
// So this has to be rejected and written with a `ret` after the loop instead,
// exactly as 05_functions/28_a_loop_followed_by_a_return.cpl is. Today it is
// accepted, because there is no check at all — this is the over-permissive end
// of the same gap as 25-32, not a separate bug.

fn f n
    while 1 == 1
        if n > 0
            ret 1
        n++

fn main
    out "{f(-3)}"
